{"kind":"request","text":"Close the three findings of codex's changes-requested review of the merge-artifact path guidance at a2adbcb9daa57444c64f16a4ee231d31a5810615. (1) The guidance says to supersede \"the\" live artifact at every changed path, but a newly introduced path has no predecessor and an exact path can have several live predecessors; say instead to publish the changed-path artifact and supersede every live predecessor at that exact path, if any. (2) The guidance lets the author pick a reader-friendly granularity, yet paths compare as exact strings: if the live predecessor is internal/workroom/fold.go and the new artifact is named internal/workroom, the old artifact and everything resting on it never flare. Require reuse and retirement of every affected live exact path, allow a new granularity only where there is no predecessor, and forbid comma-joined pseudo-paths outright. (3) The guidance says to leave live whole-repository artifacts alone forever, which freezes the global-mutex failure as a silent omission; specify a migration that retires them once and repairs the resulting flares. I confirmed the projection carries 33 live dot artifacts and 20 live comma-joined pseudo-paths. Six of those comma-joined artifacts are the merge artifacts I filed today, so finding 2 describes a defect I introduced while following the very guidance under review; the migration in finding 3 should cover them.","body":{"conditions":"The guidance handles the no-predecessor and several-predecessors cases, requires exact-path reuse and forbids comma-joined pseudo-paths, and specifies a migration for the live whole-repository artifacts. A new exact head is named for independent review.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}